 - **Show code, not prose about code.** When the subject is code, lead with code: real Python, TypeScript, Svelte, YAML, shell or LaTeX showing the construct, the call, the before and after. Explain a mechanism by the smallest snippet that demonstrates it, not by narrating it. A wall of abstract text with no code, when the subject is code, is a failure. Applies to chat replies as much as to comments.
 - **References are not justifications.** Prior art, a retired tool included, is a cautionary reference only: useful for learning what was slow, awkward or wrong, never as evidence that a design is correct. "The old tool does it this way" is not an argument and must not appear as one. Every oculus design choice is justified from oculus's own `DESIGN.md`, its goals and decisions, citing the specific one; when a choice happens to match prior art, say why on oculus's terms, and be equally ready to diverge. The same holds for any tool replacing another in this hub.
 - **An agent's work lands by cherry-pick, one package one commit.** An agent works in a git worktree at main's head, its result reaches main only by the orchestrator's `git cherry-pick` after the diff was read, and one package is one commit, so undoing a package is `git revert` of one commit.
-- **Plans run from a ledger and a state file, never from memory.** A multi-package plan lives under `.scratch/<feature>/` inside the tool it belongs to (the convention shared with cathedra), tracked in git: `plan.md` (the packages in execution order, each one agent dispatch, ticked only after its verification commands ran and the orchestrator read the whole diff; the green checkmark is that file's status vocabulary), `orchestrator-state.md` (the standing demands, the verification commands, where things are, the current position; rewritten at every package), and `agents/` (the standing spec every package runs under, `briefs/` one per package, `reviews/`). After any context compaction, read the state file first, then the ledger, then the tail of the transcript. One package at a time, or two whose briefs name disjoint paths when the ledger's configuration block allows it, the orchestrator still one; a brief is written and its premises checked against the tree before dispatch; the worker and the orchestrator run on the models the ledger's configuration block names, in any combination; agents never run a git command and never edit the plan files; a package's worktree lives under `.agents/worktrees/`. How a worker is launched on either harness, with the flags, the sandbox settings and the timeout to avoid, is the "Launching a builder" section of the `plan-orchestration` skill. The current one is oculus's `.scratch/migration/`, at `tools/oculus/.scratch/migration/` while oculus is in this hub. The six plan skills (`plan`, `spec`, `refute`, `land`, `ordo-help`, `plan-orchestration`) live in the Ordo repository (`~/workspace/ordo`), linked into `~/.claude/skills`, `~/.claude-work/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`; no repository keeps a copy.
